00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:19,078
So one of the concept to understand when
we're talking about cross compiling and

184
00:12:19,078 --> 00:12:22,540
toolchains is static
versus dynamic linking.

185
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:29,270
So gcc or g++, as I mentioned before,
we're going to be invoking possesses APIs.

186
00:12:29,270 --> 00:12:35,491
And the way we'll do that with gcc or
g++ is using the glibc,

187
00:12:35,491 --> 00:12:40,051
which is the the GCC
version of the C library.

188
00:12:41,340 --> 00:12:45,731
There's two different options
about how you would link with GCC.

189
00:12:45,731 --> 00:12:50,721
One is using static linkage and in
static linkage, all of the dependencies.

190
00:12:50,721 --> 00:12:54,657
So everything that you need from the glibc
would be pulled out of that archive and

191
00:12:54,657 --> 00:12:56,551
placed directly in your executable.

192
00:12:57,640 --> 00:13:02,421
And this is contrast with with dynamic
linking, where in dynamic linking,

193
00:13:02,421 --> 00:13:07,294
your executables doesn't actually have
that content of the glibc library.

194
00:13:07,294 --> 00:13:11,668
Instead at runtime it needs to know
where it can find a copy of that

195
00:13:11,668 --> 00:13:14,989
code locally and
when it's loaded into memory,

196
00:13:14,989 --> 00:13:19,710
the content that it needs from that
library will also be accessible.

197
00:13:19,710 --> 00:13:24,617
So the benefit being here that you don't
need a separate copy on disk for every

198
00:13:24,617 --> 00:13:30,100
application that uses glibc, which pretty
much every application is going to use.

199
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:34,522
They can all use one shared file that's
located in one spot on the file system,

200
00:13:34,522 --> 00:13:37,551
not need to duplicate that
copy that content on disk.

201
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:44,211
So if you have a way to reduce
the usage of filesystem space through

202
00:13:44,211 --> 00:13:50,240
dynamic linking, then why would you
ever want to use static linking?

203
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:54,564
Well, one reason that you might want
to use static linking is when you have

204
00:13:54,564 --> 00:13:59,380
relatively few applications or maybe if
you have only a single application in your

205
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:01,507
embedded system implementation.

206
00:14:01,507 --> 00:14:06,044
And you might say, well that seems
unlikely that you'd only have a single

207
00:14:06,044 --> 00:14:11,019
application, but it turns out there's a
really useful tool that we're going to be

208
00:14:11,019 --> 00:14:13,962
using a lot this in this
course called Busybox.

209
00:14:13,962 --> 00:14:18,259
That it does exactly that that's really
it's just a single application that

210
00:14:18,259 --> 00:14:22,690
includes a whole bunch of useful Linux
command line functions all compiled into

211
00:14:22,690 --> 00:14:24,540
one single application.

212
00:14:24,540 --> 00:14:28,379
So if you only have a single application,
there's really less benefit to using

213
00:14:28,379 --> 00:14:32,351
dynamic linking because you can just
include the content directly and executed.

214
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:37,119
And the second reason you might want to
use static linkage would be if you need to

215
00:14:37,119 --> 00:14:39,940
run an application before the root
file system is available.

216
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:43,490
So when would you not have the full
root file system available?

217
00:14:43,490 --> 00:14:46,766
Typically that would happen if
you're booting up the device and

218
00:14:46,766 --> 00:14:50,407
maybe you weren't able to access
the file system content because it was

219
00:14:50,407 --> 00:14:54,540
stored on some other device that
needed specific drivers to access it.

